```js
updated(changedProperties) {
  if (changedProperties.has('error')) {
    // existing behaviour: mirror the error attribute onto each child
    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      if (this.error) el.setAttribute('error', '');
      else el.removeAttribute('error');
    });
  }

  if (changedProperties.has('validity')) {
    const isInvalid = this.validity !== 'valid';
    this.items.forEach((el) => {
      if (isInvalid) el.setAttribute('aria-invalid', 'true');
      else el.removeAttribute('aria-invalid');
    });
  }
}

render() {
  const isInvalid = this.validity !== 'valid';

  return html`
    <fieldset
      role="radiogroup"
      aria-invalid="${isInvalid ? 'true' : 'false'}"
      aria-describedby="${this.error ? 'group-error' : nothing}"
    >
      <legend><slot name="legend"></slot></legend>
      <slot></slot>
      ${this.error
        ? html`<p id="group-error" role="alert" aria-live="assertive">${this.error}</p>`
        : nothing}
    </fieldset>
  `;
}
```

Key changes:
- `aria-invalid` on the `fieldset[role="radiogroup"]` reflects `validity` (authoritative state), not just presence of an error string — so `valueMissing` (e.g. required-but-untouched) is also exposed, not only `customError`.
- The error `<p>` gets a stable `id="group-error"`, and the fieldset references it via `aria-describedby` — this gives a persistent, re-discoverable association (AT can inspect it anytime), rather than relying solely on the one-shot `aria-live` announcement, which is missed by anyone not focused there when it fires.
